From: REG-Linux <cjom@reglinux.org>
Subject: [PATCH] Acorn/Archimedes: delete ConcreteMachine's copy operations

GCC 16.1 recurses forever in its C++ front end when std::make_unique
<ConcreteMachine>() makes it implicitly declare ConcreteMachine's copy
constructor: walking the self-referential CRTP member

    InstructionSet::ARM::Executor<arm_model,
        MemoryController<ConcreteMachine, ConcreteMachine>, ConcreteMachine>

leads straight back to declaring the same constructor, until cc1plus runs
out of stack and dies with a SIGSEGV "internal compiler error" on every
architecture (it is not RISC-V specific, and not affected by -O or -std).

ConcreteMachine is never copied - it holds pointers to itself - so declare
the copy operations deleted. GCC then never synthesizes them, and the
Archimedes machine builds everywhere again.

Signed-off-by: REG-Linux <cjom@reglinux.org>
---
--- a/Machines/Acorn/Archimedes/Archimedes.cpp
+++ b/Machines/Acorn/Archimedes/Archimedes.cpp
@@ -111,6 +111,11 @@
 	}
 
 public:
+	// Never copied (it holds pointers to itself); declaring these also keeps
+	// GCC 16 from recursing forever synthesizing them for the Executor member.
+	ConcreteMachine(const ConcreteMachine &) = delete;
+	ConcreteMachine &operator=(const ConcreteMachine &) = delete;
+
 	ConcreteMachine(
 		const Analyser::Static::Acorn::ArchimedesTarget &target,
 		const ROMMachine::ROMFetcher &rom_fetcher
